This page sits in Part V ('Données préliminaires') of Grothendieck's notes on the 'équation des lacets'. The surrounding work studies the Teichmüller groups Γ̂_{0,3}, Γ̂_{1,1} together with Π_{0,3}, the profinite fundamental group of P^1 minus {0,1,∞}. The aim is to describe, axiomatically and over finite or profinite quotients, automorphisms u of SL(2,Z)^ (or GL(2,Z)^) of Grothendieck–Teichmüller type. Such a u sends ρ and σ to conjugates of their powers, with compatibility conditions involving α, β and ℓ_1. The previous page decided to rename: the group generated by Γ̂_{1,1} ≅ GL(2,Z)^ becomes 𝔾, and the closed subgroup generated by ρ, σ becomes 𝔾^+ (image of SL(2,Z)^).

Top of page (end of the discussion begun before). The involution τ' is the one that normalises both cyclic subgroups L_ρ = <ρ> and L_σ = <σ>, via τ'ρτ'^{-1} = ρ^{-1} and τ'στ'^{-1} = σ^{-1}. It is used in place of τ, which satisfies τ(ε_0) = ε_0^{-1}, τ(ε_1) = ε_1^{-1} and even τ(σ) = σ^{-1} ('very virtuous'). However τ acts on ρ less conveniently: τ(ρ) = σρ^{-1}σ^{-1}.

In matrices this can be checked with ε_0 = [[1,1],[0,1]], ε_1 = [[1,0],[1,1]], σ = [[0,-1],[1,0]], ρ = [[1,-1],[1,0]]:
- τ = diag(1,-1);
- τ' = [[0,1],[1,0]], so τ' = ±τσ.

A diagonal marginal note records drawbacks of τ'. It apparently does not respect the {0,1}-marked structure (tangential base points, Π̂_{0,3}^{{1,2}}), unlike τ. This links to the earlier 'rigidity relation' Π̂_{0,3} = Π̂_{0,3}^σ = .... It also seems not to come from the natural place in the moduli tower (M^!_{0,3}?).

Part V, 1°). Let 𝔾 be a profinite group with an epimorphism GL(2,Z)^ → 𝔾. Equivalently, 𝔾 has three topological generators ρ, σ, τ' with
- ρ^6 = σ^4 = τ'^2 = 1;
- ρ^3 = σ^2 (written -1);
- τ'ρτ'^{-1} = ρ^{-1} and τ'στ'^{-1} = σ^{-1}.

This is exactly the amalgam presentation GL(2,Z) = D_4 *_{(Z/2)^2} D_6, with dihedral groups of orders 8 and 12 amalgamated over {±1, ±τ'}. Hence a quotient of GL(2,Z)^ is the same as such a triple. 𝔾^+ = closed subgroup generated by ρ, σ = image of SL(2,Z)^.

Next page: assuming τ ∉ 𝔾^+, one gets a semidirect product 𝔾 = {1,τ}·𝔾^+ and the sign character ε_𝔾. One then defines the group Z_{ρ,σ} ⊂ Aut(𝔾^+) × Ẑ^* × {±1}^2 of 'GT-like' automorphism data u with:
- u(ε_0) = ε_0^μ;
- u(ρ) conjugate in 𝔾^+ to ρ^{ε'};
- u(σ) conjugate in 𝔾^+ to σ^{-ε}.

The point is a finite-level, axiomatic version of genus-one GT-type actions, set up so that one can compute in arbitrary finite quotients.
